# Keep shell scripts LF-only regardless of a contributor's autocrlf setting —
# a CRLF shebang (`#!/bin/sh\r`) fails with "bad interpreter" on Linux/macOS,
# which would break the one-line CLI installer piped to `sh`.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Keep SQL migrations LF-only for the same reason, but with sharper teeth:
# sqlx hashes each migration file at compile time and stores that checksum in
# the database's `_sqlx_migrations` table when the migration is applied. CRLF
# changes the bytes, so the checksum changes — and a backend built from a CRLF
# checkout then refuses to start against a database migrated by an LF build:
#   Error: migration: migration 1 was previously applied but has been modified
# The migration content is identical; only the line endings differ. This bit a
# lb7666.top deploy built from a Windows checkout (2026-08-11) and crash-looped
# the service. CI never sees it — Linux checkouts are LF either way.
*.sql text eol=lf
